That is the whole transformation.

::: {.pull-quote}
Line everyone up by what they would pay, then count who is still standing at each price.
:::

The running count is quantity, and price and quantity together are a demand curve.

It is worth pausing on why this works rather than accepting it as a trick. Each person's maximum willingness to pay is the price at which they drop out. Below it they are in, above it they are gone. So the number of buyers at any price is simply the number of people whose drop-out point is at or above it, and counting them from the top down is the fastest way to get that number at every price at once.

@fig-empirical-demand plots the result.


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![The empirical demand curve. Each step down is one respondent's maximum price.](estimating-demand_files/figure-html/fig-empirical-demand-1.png){#fig-empirical-demand fig-align='center' width=672}
:::
:::


Notice what this object is. It is not a model and it contains no assumption about anybody who was not asked. Every step down is one respondent leaving. With twelve people the staircase is coarse; with two hundred it looks very nearly like a smooth curve, because it is the same construction with smaller steps.

You do not have to take that on faith. The slider below keeps adding respondents drawn to look like the twelve you already have, and the staircase refines as they arrive.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof nresp = Inputs.range([12, 300], {value: 12, step: 1, label: "Respondents"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
// The twelve real answers, then a pool of further respondents resampled from
// them with a little jitter. Simulated, and captioned as such: they show what
// more evidence of the SAME shape would do, not what anyone actually said.
// Seeded, so the slider ADDS respondents rather than redrawing the sample.
observed = [12, 8, 15, 10, 5, 12, 18, 9, 12, 15, 8, 10]

rng = { let a = 20260814; return () => { a |= 0; a = a + 0x6D2B79F5 | 0;
  let t = Math.imul(a ^ a >>> 15, 1 | a); t = t + Math.imul(t ^ t >>> 7, 61 | t) ^ t;
  return ((t ^ t >>> 14) >>> 0) / 4294967296; } }

pool = {
  const out = observed.slice();
  while (out.length < 300) {
    const base = observed[Math.floor(rng() * observed.length)];
    const jit  = (rng() + rng() + rng() - 1.5) * 2.4;
    out.push(Math.max(1, Math.round(base + jit)));
  }
  return out;
}

sample = pool.slice(0, nresp)

staircase = {
  const counts = d3.rollups(sample, v => v.length, d => d)
    .sort((a, b) => d3.descending(a[0], b[0]));
  let run = 0;
  return counts.map(([wtp, n]) => ({wtp, quantity: (run += n)}));
}

Plot.plot({
  width: 700, height: 400, marginLeft: 62, marginBottom: 46,
  style: {background: "transparent", fontSize: "13px", color: "#6B7280"},
  x: {domain: [0, 26], label: "Price \u2192", tickFormat: d => `$${d}`, labelAnchor: "right"},
